openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:53:Bootstrap first installs Cilium, External Secrets Operator (ESO), Longhorn, and non-HA ArgoCD with Helm. Those prerequisites break scheduling, CRD, and chart-hook bootstrap cycles; wave annotations are not a substitute for installing them. Applying [bootstrap/bootstrap.yaml](../../bootstrap/bootstrap.yaml) creates the `argocd` Namespace and the `root` Application, which tracks this repository's `main` at `apps/`, in project `default`, against the local Kubernetes API.
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:55:The root discovers the parent Application manifests in [apps/root.yaml](../../apps/root.yaml) **and** the standalone [apps/anthropic-oauth-proxy.yaml](../../apps/anthropic-oauth-proxy.yaml). Do not confuse the file named `root.yaml` with the `root` Application: the Application itself is declared in bootstrap. Directory-backed parents then reconcile their own local manifests and nested chart Applications. The proxy is different: it renders `k8s` from `basher83/tailnet-microservices` `main`, rather than a directory in this repository. This is an ownership graph, not one recursive Helm release.
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:59:    B["Helm prerequisites and bootstrap.yaml"] --> R["root Application: main at apps/"]
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:101:Do not read these annotations as proof that a controller is ready, an ExternalSecret has populated a Secret, a nameserver is available, or a PVC can bind. ArgoCD Ingress and the Tailscale parent even share outer wave `4`. The committed HA chart configuration contains diff customizations, not an Application-health dependency evaluator. Bootstrap prerequisites and live readiness checks remain necessary, especially before promoting HA. See [bootstrap and triage](../operations/bootstrap-and-triage.md) and [secrets](../integrations/secrets.md).
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:105:The normal policy is automated sync with `prune: true`, `selfHeal: true`, and `ServerSideApply=true`. Many directory parents also retry five times with exponential backoff starting at `5s`, factor `2`, capped at `3m`. This is bounded retry, not a dependency-readiness guarantee. The bootstrap root itself specifies automated prune and self-heal but does not explicitly set SSA.
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:153:3. **Application deletion/finalizers:** bootstrap `root`, the directory parents in `apps/root.yaml`, and the standalone proxy carry `resources-finalizer.argocd.argoproj.io`. Cascading Application deletion is a different path from automated pruning. The inspected nested Longhorn, CoreDNS, and HA chart Application manifests do not declare that finalizer; do not infer blanket cascade behavior or immunity from the parent policy alone. In particular, the CoreDNS no-prune comment expresses a preservation intent, not permission to delete its ancestors casually.
openwiki/architecture/reconciliation.md:164:Further source guidance: [architecture](../../docs/architecture.md), [bootstrap procedure](../../README.md#bootstrap), and [reconciliation troubleshooting](../../docs/troubleshooting.md).
openwiki/testing/validation-gates.md:56:- `ci` depends on `hygiene` and `validate:gitops`, then prints `Fast gate passed`. Mise allows two jobs; do not interpret these dependencies as a mandatory serial hygiene-then-render sequence.
openwiki/testing/validation-gates.md:62:The [Fast Gate workflow](../../.github/workflows/fast-gate.yml) runs on pull requests and pushes to `main` and `renovate/**`. It has read-only repository contents permission, uses `ubuntu-latest`, and has a ten-minute timeout. It checks out the repository, installs the Mise tools through `jdx/mise-action` with Mise `2026.11.6` and caching, then runs `mise run ci`. A newer run cancels an in-progress run with the same workflow/ref concurrency group.
openwiki/testing/validation-gates.md:140:Keep promotion evidence separate from CI status. For reconciliation and manual-sync boundaries, see [Reconciliation](../architecture/reconciliation.md), [Application changes](../workflows/application-changes.md), and [Bootstrap and triage](../operations/bootstrap-and-triage.md).
openwiki/integrations/tailscale-networking.md:174:The repository's [GitOps validator](../../scripts/validate-gitops.sh) renders exact-pinned Helm Applications and schema-checks their output. It is useful for chart changes, but is not a live test of DNS forwarding, operator-generated labels, TLS, or ClusterIP bypass prevention. Use [Bootstrap and triage](../operations/bootstrap-and-triage.md) and the [source networking guide](../../docs/tailscale-networking.md) alongside these focused checks.
openwiki/integrations/secrets.md:55:The ownership rule is **Infisical → External Secrets Operator (ESO) → Kubernetes Secret → workload**. Git holds references, key mappings, and chart configuration, never secret values. The only manually created Secret exception is ESO's universal-auth bootstrap credentials; application credentials belong in Infisical, not ad hoc Kubernetes Secrets.
openwiki/integrations/secrets.md:59:[ClusterSecretStores](../../apps/external-secrets/clustersecretstore.yaml) all use `https://app.infisical.com`, project `mothership-s0-ew`, environment `prod`. Each store fixes a different `secretsPath`, so an ExternalSecret selects a path by choosing its store and then names keys within that scope. These are provider lookup scopes, not evidence of separate Infisical identities or namespace-level authorization: all six stores reference the same bootstrap Secret.
